																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13886219/greenlight-request-frozen-corpse-and-nervous-system">Greenlight request- Frozen Corpse and Nervous System</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4832514">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I guess I'll explain what I can about the narrative; I wasn't going to, since this is only a brief initial pitch, but hopefully you'll find this to be interesting at least.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you want greenlights on an idea, you have to pitch the whole idea and not just some of it.</p>
<hr />
<p>My concern with the narrative here is that it feels&#8230;lacking in closure? It might work if you can get a reader to feel invested in the SCP and so it being frozen indefinitely ends up feeling like a huge sad moment; but it feels like it might be hard to land that in a way that works? Particularly since the freezing here is implied to be perfect (i.e., that they've circumvented the usual issues where ice shards form within a body and rupture the cells); and so in turn, there's an implication that even if they wait a really long time, they could still wake her up?</p>
<p>The other issue with doing a sad ending here is that it feels like it might be really hard to get a reader to emphasise with the legs here; like&#8230;it's not like they can really talk and be a character and so you're likely going to have to rely on characterising them via their actions and mannerisms.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
I'm willing to <span style="color: green">greenlight</span> this though because I don't think either thing here is a major issue, and I think it'll be easier to tell if it works via a draft y'know? This said: there's a possibility this might work best as a tale and I'd urge you to seriously consider that. It'd allow you to spend a lot more time in the researchers mind if this was like&#8230;a write up of that investigation happening and his reaction to stuff, than if like, he has to formally write it up in a SCP file, y'know?
							</div>

								
					
